And then I say to Toby, you know what?
然后我对托比说，你知道吗？

1189
00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:29.900
Michael was right.
迈克尔说得对。

1190
00:55:30.240 --> 00:55:31.360
You are pathetic.
你真是个可怜虫。

1191
00:55:33.680 --> 00:55:34.200
Wow.
哇。

1192
00:55:34.580 --> 00:55:35.440
I know.
我知道。

1193
00:55:36.160 --> 00:55:43.280
Well, so, you know, Angela, what you didn't see in deleted scenes is that she goes to Toby for help and he's no help.
嗯，所以，你知道，安吉拉，你在删减片段里没看到的是，她去找托比寻求帮助，但他一点忙也帮不上。

1194
00:55:43.280 --> 00:55:47.740
So now, back in the real scenes, she goes to Dwight.
所以现在，回到正片里，她去找了德怀特。

1195
00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:49.100
But there would have been a progression.
但本来应该有一个过程的。

1196
00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:49.900
She was mad.
她很生气。

1197
00:55:50.020 --> 00:55:50.780
She went to Toby.
她去找了托比。

1198
00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:51.740
He didn't help her.
他没帮她。

1199
00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:53.220
Now she goes to Dwight.
现在她去找德怀特了。

1200
00:55:53.280 --> 00:55:53.640
Yes.
是的。

1201
00:55:53.840 --> 00:55:56.000
As number three, how can he help her?
作为三号人物，他怎么能帮她呢？

1202
00:55:56.580 --> 00:56:01.700
He walks over to reception where Pam and Karen are on Pam's computer.
他走到前台，帕姆和凯伦正围在帕姆的电脑前。

1203
00:56:01.700 --> 00:56:05.540
And he says, I am dismantling your party.
他说，我要解散你们的派对。

1204
00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:11.700
I'm dismantling your committee and I'm canceling your party as ranking number three.
作为排名第三的人，我要解散你们的委员会，并取消你们的派对。

1205
00:56:11.880 --> 00:56:14.500
But then Jim walks up as ranking number two.
但这时吉姆走过来，他是排名第二的人。

1206
00:56:15.020 --> 00:56:20.160
And he assembles a one-man committee to determine the validity of the party planning committees.
他组建了一个单人委员会，来裁定派对策划委员会的合法性。

1207
00:56:20.480 --> 00:56:24.720
Wait, I have to tell you, this is the actual name of his committee.
等等，我得告诉你，这是他委员会的真实名称。

1208
00:56:24.860 --> 00:56:25.000
Ready?
准备好了吗？

1209
00:56:25.120 --> 00:56:26.460
I love how many committees there are.
我喜欢这里有这么多委员会。

1210
00:56:26.580 --> 00:56:27.820
So there's the party planning committee.
所以有派对策划委员会。

1211
00:56:28.020 --> 00:56:29.760
There's the committee to plan parties.
还有派对筹备委员会。

1212
00:56:29.760 --> 00:56:36.580
Now, Jim is the sole member of the committee to determine the validity of the two committees.
现在，吉姆是裁定这两个委员会合法性委员会的唯一成员。

1213
00:56:38.180 --> 00:56:42.440
I love that.
我喜欢这个。

1214
00:56:42.700 --> 00:56:43.940
And Jenna, what does he decide?
那么珍娜，他决定了什么？

1215
00:56:44.520 --> 00:56:47.420
He decides in favor of Karen and Pam.
他支持凯伦和帕姆。

1216
00:56:47.680 --> 00:56:51.080
He says their committee to plan parties can stand.
他说她们的派对筹备委员会可以保留。

1217
00:56:51.760 --> 00:56:58.080
Dwight asks if he could please become a member of the committee to determine the validity of the two committees.
德怀特问他是否可以加入裁定这两个委员会合法性的委员会。

1218
00:56:58.600 --> 00:57:00.020
And Jim says...
吉姆说……

1219
00:57:00.020 --> 00:57:00.880
Permission denied.
申请驳回。

1220
00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:01.860
There you go.
就是这样。

1221
00:57:02.320 --> 00:57:05.940
So now Michael is still moping in his office.
现在迈克尔还在办公室里闷闷不乐。

1222
00:57:06.180 --> 00:57:10.300
And Andy invites him to lunch at Benihana to cheer him up.
安迪邀请他去红花铁板烧吃午饭，想让他振作起来。

1223
00:57:11.020 --> 00:57:14.840
And then Michael invites Jim, Ryan, and Dwight.
然后迈克尔又邀请了吉姆、瑞安和德怀特。

1224
00:57:15.220 --> 00:57:19.360
But Ryan has a delicious list of excuses as to why he cannot go.
但瑞安有一长串绝妙的借口，说明他为什么不能去。

1225
00:57:19.540 --> 00:57:21.480
He rattles them all off in a row.
他一口气全说了出来。

1226
00:57:21.640 --> 00:57:23.360
He says, I'm not feeling well.
他说，我感觉不太舒服。

1227
00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:24.560
I've got a ton of work to do.
我还有一大堆工作要做。

1228
00:57:25.080 --> 00:57:25.840
MSG allergy.
我对味精过敏。

1229
00:57:26.560 --> 00:57:28.360
I just ate there last night.
我昨晚刚在那儿吃过。

1230
00:57:29.040 --> 00:57:32.540
And Jim is like, wow, thanks for taking all of the excuses.
吉姆说，哇，谢谢你把借口都用光了。

1231
00:57:32.900 --> 00:57:37.220
And then Ryan goes, doctor appointment, car trouble, planter warts, granddad fought in World War II.
然后瑞安又说，医生预约、车子出毛病、脚底疣、我爷爷参加过二战。

1232
00:57:38.560 --> 00:57:39.880
He's got like a ton of them.
他有一大堆这种借口。

1233
00:57:39.880 --> 00:57:41.640
He keeps them on his phone.
他把它们存在手机里。

1234
00:57:41.840 --> 00:57:43.440
He's like, I keep them on my phone.
他说，我把它们存在手机里。

1235
00:57:43.580 --> 00:57:45.500
You have to like stay sharp, Jim.
你得保持敏锐，吉姆。

1236
00:57:45.980 --> 00:57:46.160
Yeah.
是啊。

1237
00:57:46.420 --> 00:57:47.080
Step it up, Jim.
加把劲，吉姆。

1238
00:57:47.320 --> 00:57:47.840
Welcome back.
欢迎回来。

1239
00:57:48.080 --> 00:57:55.520
We had a fan catch from Casey Hilton who wants us to know that there is no Benihana in Scranton or even near Scranton.
我们收到粉丝凯西·希尔顿的反馈，她想让我们知道斯克兰顿或者斯克兰顿附近根本没有红花铁板烧。

1240
00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:57.720
I looked that up.
我查过了。

1241
00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:05.540
They have one in Pittsburgh, which is a four and a half hour drive, and one in Plymouth Meeting, which is a one hour and 45 minute drive.
匹兹堡有一家，开车要四个半小时，普利茅斯会议中心也有一家，开车要一小时四十五分钟。

1242
00:58:05.760 --> 00:58:07.000
We didn't even try.
我们甚至都没试着去找。

1243
00:58:07.360 --> 00:58:10.620
We just were like, OK, clearly there's not one there.
我们当时就想，好吧，显然那里没有。

1244
00:58:10.780 --> 00:58:12.800
We're just we're just going to write it in anyway.
我们还是决定把它写进去。

1245
00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:14.900
So they're getting their table at Benihana.
所以他们在红花铁板烧入座了。

1246
00:58:15.120 --> 00:58:16.200
And you guys know how it is.
你们都知道那是怎么回事。

1247
00:58:16.300 --> 00:58:18.640
You all sit around like the stovetop, right?
大家都围坐在炉台边，对吧？

1248
00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:19.700
Yeah.
是啊。

1249
00:58:19.700 --> 00:58:26.160
And and so Michael and Andy sit together and Jim and then there's a seat next to Jim.
迈克尔和安迪坐在一起，然后是吉姆，吉姆旁边还有一个空位。

1250
00:58:26.260 --> 00:58:32.300
But a couple scoochies in there before Dwight can get in there because Dwight and Andy were like wrestling for the seat next to Michael.
但在德怀特坐下之前，一对情侣挤了进去，因为德怀特和安迪当时在争抢迈克尔旁边的座位。

1251
00:58:32.620 --> 00:58:34.160
And now there's a couple there.
现在那里有一对情侣。

1252
00:58:34.260 --> 00:58:41.500
And you guys that have watched the podcast before, you will know Stephen Socks, our stand in, is the man in the couple.
看过我们播客的朋友们应该知道，我们的替身演员斯蒂芬·索克斯就是那对情侣中的男方。

1253
00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:43.600
He got to be in the scene.
他有机会出镜了。

1254
00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:45.120
And I just love that.
我真是太喜欢这一点了。

1255
00:58:45.120 --> 00:58:51.040
Well, you know, on IMDb, he is credited as playing the role of Justin Spitzer.
你知道吗，在IMDb上，他被标注为饰演贾斯汀·斯皮策这个角色。

1256
00:58:51.720 --> 00:59:02.480
The name that they gave to the character sitting at the table is our writer's name, Justin Spitzer, played by our stand in Stephen Socks.
他们给坐在桌边那个角色起的名字，其实就是我们编剧贾斯汀·斯皮策的名字，而这个角色是由我们的替身斯蒂芬·索克斯扮演的。

1257
00:59:03.040 --> 00:59:04.180
I love that.
我太喜欢这个安排了。

1258
00:59:04.340 --> 00:59:04.820
Here's the thing.
问题是这样的。

1259
00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:09.960
Now Dwight's on the other end of the table and he's going to have to shout throughout the rest of the time there at Benihana.
现在德怀特坐在桌子的另一头，在贝尼哈纳餐厅剩下的时间里，他不得不一直大喊大叫。

1260
00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:10.840
He has to keep shouting.
他必须不停地喊。

1261
00:59:11.060 --> 00:59:13.580
And he's really annoying this couple in between.
而且他真的让中间那对情侣感到很烦。

1262
00:59:13.580 --> 00:59:20.920
And Jenna, I was just like, I know it's for comedy's sake, but you know that couple would have been like, do you want to trade seats so you're next to your friend?
珍娜，我当时就在想，我知道这是为了喜剧效果，但那对情侣肯定会说：“你想换个座位跟你朋友坐一起吗？”

1263
00:59:20.920 --> 00:59:22.000
I thought the same thing.
我也这么想。

1264
00:59:22.300 --> 00:59:24.160
I thought the same thing.
我也这么想。

1265
00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:27.580
What, they want this guy shouting over them through the whole meal?
怎么，他们想让这家伙在整顿饭期间对着他们大喊大叫吗？

1266
00:59:28.020 --> 00:59:28.200
No.
不。

1267
00:59:28.200 --> 00:59:28.860
Of course not.
当然不想。

1268
00:59:29.080 --> 00:59:29.740
But you know what?
但你知道吗？

1269
00:59:29.880 --> 00:59:33.960
Maybe they had already started to get their food or something and it just felt like a huge headache.
也许他们已经开始上菜了之类的，觉得换座位太麻烦了。

1270
00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:34.740
I don't know.
我不知道。

1271
00:59:34.840 --> 00:59:36.440
But they stay.
但他们还是留下了。

1272
00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:39.700
They're like, no, we want to be in the center of the table.
他们就像是说：“不，我们想坐在桌子中间。”

1273
00:59:39.700 --> 00:59:42.000
We want to see the chef most clearly.
“我们想最清楚地看到厨师表演。”

1274
00:59:42.000 --> 00:59:45.920
I don't know what their motivation was for not letting this guy sit with his group.
我不知道他们为什么不让这家伙和他的同伴坐在一起。

1275
00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:50.040
But so Michael is still being mopey, even though they're at Benihana.
总之，迈克尔即使到了贝尼哈纳餐厅，依然闷闷不乐。

1276
00:59:50.440 --> 00:59:54.120
You know, he wants to call Carol and Andy's like, ah, let's start ordering drinks.
你知道，他想给卡罗尔打电话，而安迪却说：“啊，我们开始点酒水吧。”

1277
00:59:54.260 --> 00:59:58.120
He starts ordering this weird eggnog sake concoction.
他开始点那种奇怪的蛋奶酒清酒特调。

1278
00:59:58.280 --> 00:59:59.180
It sounds disgusting.
听起来真恶心。

1279
00:59:59.520 --> 01:00:06.180
We had a fan question from Dan Solomon who wanted to know, was that a real drink or was it invented by the writers?
我们收到了一位粉丝丹·所罗门的提问，他想知道，那是一种真正的饮料，还是编剧们编出来的？

1280
01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:08.080
It's a made-up drink.
那是编出来的饮料。

1281
01:00:08.340 --> 01:00:09.660
This is not a real drink.
这不是一种真正的饮料。

1282
01:00:10.620 --> 01:00:19.160
Angela, someone on cracked.com did an article where they made all kinds of fake cocktails from different TV shows.
安吉拉，cracked.com网站上有人写过一篇文章，他们制作了各种来自不同电视剧的虚构鸡尾酒。

1283
01:00:19.320 --> 01:00:25.880
Like they made cocktails from 30 Rock and Parks and Rec and they did a review of this drink.
比如他们制作了《我为喜剧狂》和《公园与游憩》里的鸡尾酒，还对这种饮料做了测评。

1284
01:00:25.880 --> 01:00:31.480
Just to remind you, it is one part eggnog and three parts sake.
提醒一下，它是一份蛋奶酒加三份清酒。

1285
01:00:31.900 --> 01:00:33.480
Sounds so gross.
听起来太恶心了。

1286
01:00:34.260 --> 01:00:34.780
All right.
好吧。

1287
01:00:34.880 --> 01:00:36.200
Here's what they had to say.
他们是这么评价的。

1288
01:00:36.680 --> 01:00:37.080
Quote,
引用如下：

1289
01:00:38.240 --> 01:00:44.780
It looks like so many different disgusting things and none of them are anything I would want to put in my mouth.
“它看起来像很多种恶心的东西，而且没有一样是我愿意放进嘴里的。”

1290
01:00:45.780 --> 01:00:50.060
You can actually hear the eggnog curdling in the glass.
“你甚至能听到蛋奶酒在杯子里凝结的声音。”

1291
01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:51.000
Gross.
恶心。

1292
01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:56.540
Then they said, it's not the worst thing I've ever had, but it is far from the beSt.
然后他们说：“这不是我喝过最糟糕的东西，但也绝非佳品。”

1293
01:00:56.780 --> 01:00:59.900
The real problem lies in the aftertaste.
“真正的问题在于余味。”

1294
01:01:00.740 --> 01:01:06.660
Both of these drinks have a tendency to linger on the breath by themselves and neither is very pleasant.
“这两种饮料本身都有留在呼吸里的倾向，而且味道都不怎么好。”

1295
01:01:06.980 --> 01:01:10.280
When mixed together, they make your mouth taste like toes.
“混合在一起后，喝起来就像脚趾头的味道。”

1296
01:01:11.160 --> 01:01:18.420
I wish I could say something nice about this drink, but every time I attempt it, I suddenly forget all the good in my life.
“我真想对这种饮料说点好话，但每次我尝试时，我都会突然忘记生活中所有美好的事物。”

1297
01:01:18.580 --> 01:01:19.660
Oh my God.
天哪。

1298
01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:25.000
Well, what I want to know now is what were those guys actually drinking on the set?
好吧，我现在想知道的是，他们在片场到底喝的是什么？

1299
01:01:25.260 --> 01:01:28.320
Were they drinking water mixed with eggnog?
他们喝的是水加蛋奶酒吗？

1300
01:01:28.460 --> 01:01:30.400
Were they just drinking eggnog?
还是他们只是在喝蛋奶酒？

1301
01:01:31.180 --> 01:01:32.100
I don't know.
我不知道。

1302
01:01:32.340 --> 01:01:34.780
I don't like eggnog, so I'm not a big eggnog person.
我不喜欢蛋奶酒，所以我不怎么喝那个。

1303
01:01:35.380 --> 01:01:40.540
I liked it as a kid, but as an adult, it's just, it's very thick.
小时候我挺喜欢的，但长大后觉得它太浓稠了。

1304
01:01:40.940 --> 01:01:42.520
It's like drinking a dessert.
就像在喝甜点一样。

1305
01:01:42.840 --> 01:01:43.960
It's so sweet.
太甜了。

1306
01:01:44.200 --> 01:01:44.380
Yeah.
是啊。

1307
01:01:44.540 --> 01:01:48.060
We should go back because I need to talk to you a little bit about this scene with Roy.
我们得回去，因为我需要和你聊聊罗伊的那场戏。

1308
01:01:48.800 --> 01:01:49.320
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1309
01:01:50.200 --> 01:01:51.780
So Roy comes in.
罗伊进来了。

1310
01:01:51.940 --> 01:01:52.600
He's looking cute.
他看起来很帅。

1311
01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:54.620
He clearly just wants to talk to Pam.
他显然只是想和帕姆说话。

1312
01:01:54.880 --> 01:01:58.600
He's asking her about her gift wrapping techniques.
他问她关于包装礼物的技巧。

1313
01:01:58.600 --> 01:02:02.700
And she says she only uses three pieces of tape.
她说她只用了三块胶带。

1314
01:02:02.700 --> 01:02:04.020
Are you crazy?
你疯了吗？

1315
01:02:04.700 --> 01:02:04.860
Yeah.
是啊。

1316
01:02:05.860 --> 01:02:18.380
Well, Angela, Adrian Thornblom, Sidney Layton, and Elisa Schroeder, and Kelly Lubert wrote in to say, how on earth can you wrap a gift with only three pieces of tape?
安吉拉、阿德里安·索恩布洛姆、西德尼·雷顿、埃莉萨·施罗德还有凯莉·卢伯特都写信问，到底怎么可能只用三块胶带就包好一个礼物？

1317
01:02:19.460 --> 01:02:19.900
How?
怎么做到的？

1318
01:02:20.560 --> 01:02:20.700
How?
怎么做到的？

1319
01:02:20.700 --> 01:02:20.800
Yeah.
是啊。

1320
01:02:20.980 --> 01:02:23.200
They are all saying there must be at least four.
他们都说至少得用四块才行。

1321
01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:26.000
Or why is Pam giving such bad advice here?
或者说，为什么帕姆在这里给出了这么烂的建议？

1322
01:02:27.200 --> 01:02:28.820
Guys, it can be done.
伙计们，这是可以做到的。

1323
01:02:29.180 --> 01:02:31.720
You can wrap a gift with three pieces of tape.
你确实可以用三块胶带包好一个礼物。

1324
01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:34.620
I personally, Jenna Fisher, can't do it.
我个人，也就是珍娜·费舍，是做不到的。

1325
01:02:34.820 --> 01:02:38.360
I use more than three pieces of tape when I wrap a gift.
我包礼物时用的胶带远不止三块。

1326
01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:48.900
But because Pam says this in this episode, every Christmas, when I, Jenna Fisher, give a gift and I use more than three pieces of tape, people comment on it.
但因为帕姆在这一集里这么说了，所以每年圣诞节，当我——珍娜·费舍——送礼物用了超过三块胶带时，人们就会对此评头论足。

1327
01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:49.520
Oh, no.
噢，不。

1328
01:02:49.700 --> 01:02:50.480
Do they really?
真的吗？

1329
01:02:50.760 --> 01:02:51.100
Yes.
真的。

1330
01:02:51.240 --> 01:02:51.420
Yes.
是的。

1331
01:02:51.420 --> 01:02:53.960
They're like, use more than three pieces of tape, eh?
他们会说，哟，用了不止三块胶带啊？

1332
01:02:54.160 --> 01:02:56.420
And I'm like, I am not Pam.
我就说，我不是帕姆。

1333
01:02:56.640 --> 01:02:57.540
I can't wrap.
我不会包礼物。

1334
01:02:57.680 --> 01:02:58.660
Apparently, Pam can.
显然，帕姆会。

1335
01:02:58.920 --> 01:03:02.800
You can watch a ton of YouTube videos on how exactly Pam does it.
你可以去YouTube上看一大堆视频，看看帕姆到底是怎么做到的。

1336
01:03:03.240 --> 01:03:07.960
Many people have made videos on how to wrap a gift with only three pieces of tape so it can be done.
很多人都拍了视频演示如何只用三块胶带包礼物，所以这确实可行。

1337
01:03:08.540 --> 01:03:08.980
But.
但是。

1338
01:03:09.220 --> 01:03:10.100
Hold up, lady.
等等，女士。

1339
01:03:10.600 --> 01:03:10.740
Yeah.
是啊。

1340
01:03:10.860 --> 01:03:15.460
People commenting on your tape, that's got to be in your family.
人们评论你的胶带用量，那肯定是在你家里发生的吧。

1341
01:03:15.460 --> 01:03:18.920
Because who you're giving, at Christmas, you're like, with your family.
因为圣诞节你送礼物的时候，肯定是在和家人在一起。

1342
01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:19.420
So.
所以。

1343
01:03:19.540 --> 01:03:19.620
Yeah.
是啊。

1344
01:03:19.700 --> 01:03:23.420
You've got like some aunt that's like, oh, you use more than three pieces.
你肯定有那种阿姨会说，噢，你用了不止三块胶带呀。

1345
01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:24.440
Yeah.
是啊。

1346
01:03:24.680 --> 01:03:24.980
Yeah.
是啊。

1347
01:03:24.980 --> 01:03:25.960
I get grief.
我被念叨了。

1348
01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:29.580
I get grief from my family and some friends.
我的家人和一些朋友会因为这个念叨我。

1349
01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:31.240
For not being your character.
就因为我不是我演的那个角色。

1350
01:03:32.240 --> 01:03:32.760
Yeah.
是啊。

1351
01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:39.300
I mean, that's the tricky thing about having a character that you played for a long time who makes grand declarations.
我的意思是，饰演一个长期发表宏大宣言的角色，这确实是个棘手的问题。

1352
01:03:39.560 --> 01:03:42.000
It gets superimposed onto me.
这会投射到我身上。

1353
01:03:43.320 --> 01:03:44.280
But, you know.
但是，你知道的。

1354
01:03:44.280 --> 01:03:47.700
Do you know what I get almost every single year from a family member?
你知道我几乎每年都会从家人那里收到什么吗？

1355
01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:49.140
What?
什么？

1356
01:03:49.140 --> 01:03:53.560
I get at least one gift that has a cat on it.
我至少会收到一件印有猫咪图案的礼物。

1357
01:03:54.280 --> 01:03:54.440
Yeah.
是啊。

1358
01:03:55.720 --> 01:03:56.800
I like cats, Jenna.
我喜欢猫，珍娜。

1359
01:03:56.800 --> 01:03:58.280
Because Angela Martin likes cats.
因为安吉拉·马丁喜欢猫。

1360
01:03:58.420 --> 01:03:58.620
Yeah.
是啊。

1361
01:03:58.660 --> 01:03:59.840
I like cats.
我是喜欢猫。

1362
01:03:59.980 --> 01:04:03.100
I don't need a gift every year with a cat on it, guys.
但我不需要每年都收到印着猫的礼物，伙计们。

1363
01:04:03.540 --> 01:04:05.120
From my own family.
还是来自我的家人。

1364
01:04:05.420 --> 01:04:05.900
Yeah.
是啊。

1365
01:04:05.900 --> 01:04:21.160
My favorite is more than once, more than once, I have gotten a cat calendar and there's usually a photo of a cat hanging for dear life from a tree and it says, hang in there.
我最无语的是，不止一次，我不止一次收到过猫咪挂历，上面通常是一只猫拼命挂在树枝上，写着“坚持住”。

1366
01:04:21.640 --> 01:04:22.260
Yeah.
是啊。

1367
01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:24.560
I've gotten that calendar more than once.
那种挂历我收到过不止一次。

1368
01:04:24.640 --> 01:04:25.400
Oh, no.
噢，不。

1369
01:04:28.640 --> 01:04:29.840
Okay, wait, wait, wait.
好吧，等等，等等。

1370
01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:31.980
We have to get back to the scene because they're flirting.
我们得回到剧情里，因为他们在调情。

1371
01:04:32.260 --> 01:04:37.740
And then Karen sees the flirt, sees Roy and is like, he's cute.
然后凯伦看到了这一幕，看到了罗伊，心想，他挺帅的。

1372
01:04:37.900 --> 01:04:38.640
You should date him.
你应该和他约会。

1373
01:04:38.640 --> 01:04:42.720
And then Pam does not say a word.
结果帕姆一句话也没说。

1374
01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:44.460
She doesn't own up.
她没有承认。

1375
01:04:44.580 --> 01:04:45.720
And she just says, maybe.
她只是说，也许吧。

1376
01:04:46.380 --> 01:04:46.560
Well,
嗯，

1377
01:04:46.700 --> 01:04:59.960
I feel like Pam can't tell Karen that she used to be with Roy without it opening up a lot of questions that might lead her to having to tell her things that she knows Jim hasn't told her.
我觉得帕姆没法告诉凯伦她曾经和罗伊在一起过，因为这会引出一连串问题，可能会导致她不得不说出一些吉姆还没告诉凯伦的事。

1378
01:04:59.960 --> 01:05:00.760
I agree.
我同意。

1379
01:05:01.300 --> 01:05:07.100
Well, lady, I think we should pause our breakdown for this episode for a moment because this is kind of the halfway mark.
好吧，女士，我觉得我们应该暂停一下这集的剧情解析，因为现在差不多到中点位置了。

1380
01:05:07.100 --> 01:05:11.520
And we thought that this would be a good place to play our interview with Jen Salata.
我们觉得这正好可以播放我们对简·萨拉塔的采访。

1381
01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:16.980
Yeah, I think it would be great to hear from the writer of this episode before we dig any deeper.
是啊，我觉得在深入探讨之前，听听这集的编剧怎么说会很棒。

1382
01:05:17.540 --> 01:05:19.560
All right, Sam, will you play the interview with Jen?
好的，萨姆，能播放一下简的采访吗？

1383
01:05:21.260 --> 01:05:22.580
Hi, Jen Salata.
嗨，简·萨拉塔。

1384
01:05:22.880 --> 01:05:23.680
Hi, Jen.
嗨，简。

1385
01:05:23.900 --> 01:05:24.740
Guys look gorgeous.
你们看起来美极了。

1386
01:05:24.880 --> 01:05:27.920
You look, it literally looks like time stopped since I last saw you.
你们看起来，简直就像我上次见到你们之后时间就静止了一样。

1387
01:05:28.220 --> 01:05:29.060
I love you.
我爱你们。

1388
01:05:29.060 --> 01:05:30.260
You stop it.
别这样。

1389
01:05:30.380 --> 01:05:31.040
I'm so excited.
我太兴奋了。

1390
01:05:31.520 --> 01:05:39.600
My favorite guests are when we have writers on because you guys have this perspective that is totally different from the actors.
我最喜欢邀请编剧来做客，因为你们的视角和演员完全不同。

1391
01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:42.100
And I could just listen to you guys talk forever.
我真的可以一直听你们聊下去。

1392
01:05:42.360 --> 01:05:44.780
That's my it's my favorite side of the story.
那是我最喜欢的故事侧面。

1393
01:05:45.400 --> 01:05:48.740
That and probably Phil Shea having to make all the weird props.
还有大概就是菲尔·谢不得不制作所有那些奇怪的道具。

1394
01:05:49.560 --> 01:05:58.120
All I remember about Phil Shea is constantly when if I had to come in on like a Saturday or a Sunday because I needed to do a little work or I forgot something at the office.
关于菲尔·谢，我记得最深的就是，如果我因为需要加班或者忘了拿东西而在周六或周日去办公室，

1395
01:05:58.120 --> 01:05:59.400
He was always there.
他总是在那儿。

1396
01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:00.880
He was always there making a prop.
他总是在那儿制作道具。

1397
01:06:01.040 --> 01:06:02.760
Like he just never stopped working.
就像他永远不会停止工作一样。

1398
01:06:03.060 --> 01:06:04.240
He was just brilliant.
他真是太天才了。

1399
01:06:05.540 --> 01:06:08.420
Yeah, my memory of him is him running.
是啊，我对他的记忆就是他一直在跑。

1400
01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:12.440
He was always like like across the place and he had things in his hand.
他总是像这样在各个地方穿梭，手里还拿着东西。

1401
01:06:12.440 --> 01:06:16.520
And I'd be like, hey, I'll be like, hey, I just got to attach this hat to the squirrel.
然后我会说，嘿，我会说，嘿，我得把这顶帽子戴到松鼠头上。

1402
01:06:16.700 --> 01:06:17.920
And then I'm like, what?
然后我就想，什么？

1403
01:06:18.960 --> 01:06:22.800
What made me laugh when you told me that I forgot that he presented everything on a tray.
你跟我说他把所有东西都放在托盘上呈上来的时候，我忘了那段，但那真的让我笑喷了。

1404
01:06:22.800 --> 01:06:26.920
And the bird thing made me laugh so hard because I'm sure that is.
还有关于鸟的那段也让我笑得不行，因为我肯定事实就是那样。

1405
01:06:26.920 --> 01:06:29.000
I can imagine that I can picture that.
我能想象得到，我脑子里都有画面了。

1406
01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:36.180
And I wanted to tell you that when I was listening to the bird stories that you guys were telling from your own home,
我想告诉你，当我听你们讲各自家里发生的鸟的故事时，

1407
01:06:36.640 --> 01:06:44.180
but I Googled it and I found out that there was an article about why we're seeing dead birds and birds that are struggling now.
我上网搜了一下，发现有一篇文章解释了为什么我们现在会看到死鸟和挣扎中的鸟。

1408
01:06:44.320 --> 01:06:45.440
It's because of quarantine.
是因为隔离。

1409
01:06:45.820 --> 01:06:47.320
It was the most interesting article.
那篇文章真的非常有趣。

1410
01:06:47.320 --> 01:06:57.880
They said that we are just around and we're noticing nature more and we're more aware because we're not like in our offices all day long so that unfortunately this is a time where birds struggle,
他们说，我们只是因为待在家里，所以更多地注意到了大自然，也更有意识了，因为我们不用整天待在办公室里，所以不幸的是，这段时间正是鸟类艰难求生的时期，

1411
01:06:58.080 --> 01:06:59.560
but we don't usually see it.
但我们平时通常注意不到。

1412
01:06:59.740 --> 01:07:05.800
So I don't know if that's like yay or boo or somewhere in between, but I thought that was interesting.
所以我不知道这算是好事还是坏事，或者介于两者之间，但我认为这很有趣。

1413
01:07:06.080 --> 01:07:11.080
This is the big bird baby boom season this time of the year.
每年的这个时候都是幼鸟的生育高峰期。

1414
01:07:11.080 --> 01:07:23.980
And a big part of, cause I looked it up too, Jen, a big part of a bird's life cycle is that between the nest and being a full adult bird, they actually nest on the ground.
而且，Jen，我也查过了，鸟类生命周期中很重要的一部分是，在离开巢穴到完全成年之前，它们实际上会在地面上筑巢。

1415
01:07:23.980 --> 01:07:33.220
So they, the parents kind of kick them out of the tree and they nest under bushes or on the ground for a couple of days and their parents visit them.
所以，父母会把它们从树上赶下来，它们会在灌木丛下或地面上待几天，父母会去探望它们。

1416
01:07:33.300 --> 01:07:40.940
But it's sort of like, that's like they're going to college phase, you know, they're out of the nest, but their parents are still kind of like sending them money from time to time.
这就像是它们上大学的阶段，你知道的，它们离开了巢穴，但父母偶尔还是会给它们寄点钱。

1417
01:07:41.080 --> 01:07:50.620
And so in that little period, like 80% of birds die because they are, you know, peril meets them.
在那段时期，大约80%的鸟会死亡，因为它们会遇到危险。

1418
01:07:50.860 --> 01:07:57.020
Maybe it's, you know, a raccoon or a cat or something, a squirrel.
也许是浣熊、猫或者其他什么东西，比如松鼠。

1419
01:07:57.560 --> 01:07:57.680
Yeah.
是啊。

1420
01:07:57.900 --> 01:08:01.400
This is the part of their life that where they meet the most doom.
这是它们一生中最容易遭遇不幸的阶段。

1421
01:08:02.240 --> 01:08:03.540
Oh, this is sad.
噢，这太令人难过了。

1422
01:08:03.620 --> 01:08:09.160
But I read that somebody rescued another person, like a bird fell into a pool, but they rescued it.
但我读到有人救了另一只鸟，比如有只鸟掉进了泳池，但他们把它救了上来。

1423
01:08:09.160 --> 01:08:14.960
So it's like, there may be more rescues happening because we're more, uh, aware and vigilant right now.
所以，也许现在会有更多的救援发生，因为我们变得更加关注和警觉了。

1424
01:08:15.560 --> 01:08:26.500
Cut to next year, an article that says a rise in birds due to people intervening and saving them has now bled us of all of the gnats.
快进到明年，可能就会有一篇文章说，由于人们的干预和拯救，鸟类数量激增，导致我们所有的蚋虫都被吃光了。

1425
01:08:26.660 --> 01:08:28.840
And it turns out gnats were necessary.
结果发现蚋虫是必不可少的。

1426
01:08:29.200 --> 01:08:30.760
Nats were necessary.
蚋虫是必不可少的。

1427
01:08:31.400 --> 01:08:39.300
I know this is many on a Christmas and I'm sure you will deal with this, but like, I do remember reading articles that people's lives were saved because of, uh,
我知道这离圣诞节还远，但我肯定你会处理这个，但我确实记得读过文章说，人们的生命因为那个……

1428
01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:41.580
staying alive in the safety training.
在安全培训中保持生命体征。

1429
01:08:41.580 --> 01:08:49.980
Is it safety training in the episode where, um, stress relief, yeah, because I remember I wrote that.
是《安全培训》那一集吗？嗯，就是《压力缓解》那一集，对，因为我记得那是我写的。

1430
01:08:50.140 --> 01:08:56.400
I wrote that and I remember having to make sure I remember wanting to make sure it was correct in case anybody watched the show and actually used it.
那是我写的，我记得当时我必须确保内容准确，以防有人看了节目真的照着做。

1431
01:08:56.540 --> 01:09:02.980
But then I started to hear news reports that people remember it from the office and are saving people's lives from that.
但后来我开始听到新闻报道说，人们从《办公室》里记住了这些，并因此救了别人的命。

1432
01:09:03.620 --> 01:09:05.120
I've read those same articles.
我也读过那些文章。

1433
01:09:07.220 --> 01:09:08.080
It's crazy.
太疯狂了。

1434
01:09:08.700 --> 01:09:10.900
You you're saving lives, Jen Salata.
你救了人命啊，Jen Salata。

1435
01:09:10.900 --> 01:09:14.560
Well, I don't know, Jen.
嗯，我不知道，Jen。

1436
01:09:14.740 --> 01:09:19.740
We always like to ask people when they come on the podcast, how they got their job on the office.
我们总是喜欢问来参加播客的嘉宾，他们是怎么得到《办公室》这份工作的。

1437
01:09:19.980 --> 01:09:28.900
I was on the show Malcolm in the Middle, which, um, I really enjoyed that show, but I, every, every morning after the, after the office aired,
我当时在拍《马尔柯姆的一家》，我真的很喜欢那部剧，但是，在《办公室》播出的每天早上，

1438
01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:31.820
I would come in and just talk about the office forever.
我都会走进去，没完没了地谈论《办公室》。

1439
01:09:32.120 --> 01:09:34.760
And they were like, let's get to work on a Malcolm stories.
他们就会说，咱们还是聊聊《马尔柯姆》的故事吧。

1440
01:09:34.960 --> 01:09:36.080
And I was like, yeah, yeah, sure.
我就说，行，行，没问题。

1441
01:09:36.280 --> 01:09:38.240
Did you see what happened on diversity day?
你看到《多元化日》里发生什么了吗？

1442
01:09:38.240 --> 01:09:40.220
So I was a big fan.
所以我当时是个超级粉丝。

1443
01:09:40.500 --> 01:09:49.140
Um, and I actually, uh, wrote a short story about a woman who falls in love with the middle of a man.
嗯，我其实写过一个短篇故事，关于一个爱上男人腰部的女人。

1444
01:09:49.140 --> 01:09:54.760
She works in quality control in a button factory and she sees the middle of people walking by window all day long.
她在一家纽扣工厂做质量控制，整天透过窗户看着人们的腰部走过。

1445
01:09:54.760 --> 01:09:58.280
And she puts all her hopes and dreams in the middle of this man.
她把自己所有的希望和梦想都寄托在这个男人的腰部上。

1446
01:09:58.580 --> 01:10:03.100
And, oh my God, it's brilliant.
天哪，这简直太天才了。

1447
01:10:03.760 --> 01:10:04.940
It was so crazy.
这太疯狂了。

1448
01:10:05.160 --> 01:10:06.820
I had never written a short story before.
我以前从没写过短篇小说。

1449
01:10:06.940 --> 01:10:08.040
I wrote it.
我写了。

1450
01:10:08.180 --> 01:10:12.820
It took me forever to write it because I took me forever to sit down and figure out what I had to say.
我写了很久，因为我花了很长时间才坐下来想清楚自己要表达什么。

1451
01:10:12.960 --> 01:10:14.480
If any, did I have anything to say?
如果有的话，我有什么要说的吗？

1452
01:10:14.480 --> 01:10:16.340
And it kind of, anyway, I wrote it.
总之，不管怎样，我把它写出来了。

1453
01:10:16.460 --> 01:10:19.860
And then Suzanne Daniels, I found out that they have piles of scripts.
后来我发现苏珊·丹尼尔斯那里堆满了剧本。

1454
01:10:19.980 --> 01:10:24.840
The Daniels house has piles of scripts to the point where the kids have told me that they almost forget that they're scripts.
丹尼尔斯家里的剧本多到孩子们都跟我说，他们几乎忘了那是剧本了。

1455
01:10:24.960 --> 01:10:29.520
They think of them as furniture, like a coffee table, somewhere to put their legs up.
他们把剧本当成了家具，就像咖啡桌一样，可以把脚搁在上面。

1456
01:10:29.740 --> 01:10:38.240
And Suzanne Daniels, I guess, grabbed my short story, probably thinner than the rest of the scripts on the pile, and responded to it and gave it to Greg.
我想苏珊·丹尼尔斯大概是拿走了我的短篇小说，它可能比那一堆里的其他剧本都要薄，她看后很有感触，就把它交给了格雷格。

1457
01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:49.720
And then I met him at a coffee bean, had a meeting right before he was about to go to Upfronts for season two, and just probably begged to be on the show, was really excited,
后来我在一家咖啡豆咖啡馆见了他，就在他去参加第二季预展之前，我可能恳求过让他让我加入剧组，当时真的特别兴奋，

1458
01:10:49.940 --> 01:10:52.060
and just loved him, loved meeting him.
我真的很喜欢他，也很高兴能见到他。

1459
01:10:52.160 --> 01:10:53.460
And that's how it happened.
事情就是这样发生的。

1460
01:10:53.700 --> 01:10:54.760
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

1461
01:10:54.840 --> 01:10:56.860
That is such a fantastic story.
这故事太棒了。

1462
01:10:57.360 --> 01:11:02.240
Well, Jen, we have a question for you about, you know, obviously, we're talking Benihana Christmas today.
好的，简，我们有个问题想问你，当然，我们今天是在聊《班尼哈纳圣诞节》这一集。

1463
01:11:02.720 --> 01:11:05.240
What was the inspiration for this episode?
这一集的灵感来源是什么？

1464
01:11:05.240 --> 01:11:12.220
Do you remember how it came about, how you came to be the person to write it, like sort of the beginning of what was Benihana?
你还记得它是怎么来的吗？你是怎么成为这一集的编剧的？比如《班尼哈纳》最初是怎么构思的？

1465
01:11:12.420 --> 01:11:21.300
The initial idea was Michael goes through a breakup, and I can almost see myself leaping out of a chair at that moment when I came from the room saying,
最初的想法是迈克尔经历了一次分手，我几乎能看到自己当时从房间里冲出来，从椅子上跳起来说：

1466
01:11:21.540 --> 01:11:31.520
I need to have this episode because I just felt like taking Michael through a breakup is just like having, how would a 14-year-old boy deal with a breakup?
我一定要写这一集，因为我觉得让迈克尔经历分手，就像是……一个14岁的男孩会怎么处理分手呢？

1467
01:11:31.520 --> 01:11:36.080
You know, and just kind of just the riding those waves for the first time.
你知道，就是那种第一次经历情感波折的感觉。

1468
01:11:36.260 --> 01:11:45.320
Has he ever been that close to a person to have, you know, to a romantic partner to have those waves so you can really take him to the point where he's lying on the floor?
他以前有没有和某人亲近到那种程度，或者说和伴侣亲近到会有那种波折，以至于你可以把他写到那种躺在地板上的地步？

1469
01:11:45.320 --> 01:11:54.600
Or so I put in a little bit of 14-year-old boy and then a little bit of me, you know, listening to James Blunt, goodbye, my lover, and breakups that I've had.
所以我加入了一点14岁男孩的影子，还有一点我自己的经历，你知道，听着詹姆斯·布朗的《再见，我的爱人》，还有我经历过的那些分手。

1470
01:11:55.560 --> 01:11:59.780
So there was a smush of the two of us, my version of Michael's stuff.
所以这是我们两个人的结合体，我版本的迈克尔的故事。

1471
01:11:59.780 --> 01:12:12.260
And when I ended up leaving the office, which was devastatingly difficult to do, but I ended up telling Steve that I was leaving, and I'll never forget his comment to me.
当我最终离开《办公室》剧组时，那真的非常痛苦，但我还是告诉了史蒂夫我要离开，我永远不会忘记他对我说的话。

1472
01:12:12.380 --> 01:12:14.940
He said, I think this is going to be really difficult for you.
他说，我觉得这对你来说会非常困难。

1473
01:12:15.020 --> 01:12:17.640
And I said, yeah, no, definitely.
我说，是的，确实。

1474
01:12:17.880 --> 01:12:21.760
But he's like, because I think you work a lot of your own stuff out through my character.
但他接着说，因为我觉得你通过我的角色解决了很多你自己的问题。

1475
01:12:23.040 --> 01:12:28.780
I was like, there's this game that people play of, like, who are they in the office?
我当时想，人们常玩一个游戏，就是问“你在《办公室》里对应哪个角色？”

1476
01:12:28.800 --> 01:12:31.820
And I'm like, I can't be Pam or Angela or Phyllis.
我心想，我又不可能是帕姆、安吉拉或菲利斯。

1477
01:12:31.960 --> 01:12:33.200
Like, I'm Michael?
难道我是迈克尔？

1478
01:12:33.520 --> 01:12:35.060
Michael thinks I'm Michael?
迈克尔觉得我是迈克尔？

1479
01:12:37.220 --> 01:12:38.740
Steve was aware.
史蒂夫心里清楚。

1480
01:12:39.000 --> 01:12:41.540
Steve was aware when it was a ginsalata script.
当那是金萨拉塔的剧本时，史蒂夫心里就清楚了。

1481
01:12:41.560 --> 01:12:44.640
Oh, well, we're going to work out some issues this week.
哦，好吧，我们这周要解决一些问题了。

1482
01:12:44.960 --> 01:12:45.380
Yes.
没错。

1483
01:12:53.680 --> 01:12:59.500
I think my first breakup with a boyfriend was late, you know, mid-twenties, you know, just experiencing that.
我想我第一次和男朋友分手是在二十五六岁的时候，你知道，就是那种体验。

1484
01:12:59.740 --> 01:13:04.480
And then also just having, you know, just thinking of a young, a younger boy going through it.
还有就是，你知道，想到一个年轻男孩经历这些的感觉。

1485
01:13:04.580 --> 01:13:05.520
And I just left.
然后我就离开了。

1486
01:13:05.680 --> 01:13:06.760
I wanted it so badly.
我太想要这个机会了。

1487
01:13:06.760 --> 01:13:09.660
So I think I probably just wrestled someone to the ground for it.
所以我大概是把别人打倒在地才抢到这个机会的。

1488
01:13:10.020 --> 01:13:10.720
Oh, my God.
天哪。

1489
01:13:11.100 --> 01:13:17.880
Well, Jen, I would love to talk to you about the Party Planning Committee versus the Committee to Plan Parties.
简，我很想和你聊聊“派对策划委员会”和“派对策划委员会”之间的较量。

1490
01:13:18.120 --> 01:13:19.100
What the hell?
搞什么鬼？

1491
01:13:19.100 --> 01:13:25.300
You were saying on a previous podcast, Jenna, that I really am drawn to Angela and writing for Angela.
珍娜，你在之前的播客里说过，你真的很喜欢安吉拉，也喜欢为安吉拉写剧本。

1492
01:13:25.600 --> 01:13:32.380
And there was something, I was starting to think about why that was, besides just being brilliant and it's so funny and it's just fun to write for you.
我开始思考为什么会这样，除了因为你才华横溢、非常有趣，而且为你写剧本很有意思之外。

1493
01:13:32.500 --> 01:13:39.560
But I was thinking, I can't stand sometimes rules and bureaucracy and all of that stuff.
我还在想，有时候我真的受不了那些规则、官僚主义之类的东西。

1494
01:13:39.720 --> 01:13:45.900
And I feel like your character represented so many of those lines and things you can't do and limits.
我觉得你的角色代表了太多那些条条框框、不能做的事和限制。

1495
01:13:45.900 --> 01:13:53.960
So somehow it was like cathartic for me to go deep in there and like have you push those lines with people.
所以对我来说，深入挖掘这些，让你去挑战那些底线，反而是一种宣泄。

1496
01:13:54.200 --> 01:13:57.600
You know, comedy writers oftentimes want to push against authority and rules.
你知道，喜剧编剧通常都想挑战权威和规则。

1497
01:13:57.620 --> 01:13:59.640
And so you were, you represented that.
而你，你代表了这些。

1498
01:13:59.820 --> 01:14:02.220
So I just kind of love to give you that.
所以我很喜欢给你安排这些情节。

1499
01:14:02.340 --> 01:14:04.020
So then what do you do to your character?
那么你对你的角色做了什么？

1500
01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:11.940
You threaten her with a rival party and just kind of up the stakes, a rival planning committee and up the stakes.
你用一个竞争对手派对威胁她，提高了赌注，一个竞争对手策划委员会，提高了赌注。

1501
01:14:11.940 --> 01:14:20.140
And have you kind of lose it when you run out to try to get the permission from Dwight that the party needs to start now.
你当时是不是有点失控了，因为你急着跑去想让德怀特批准派对现在就开始。

1502
01:14:20.760 --> 01:14:30.140
It's just like the intensity with which you're clinging to the fact that you need to maintain the balance of your structure is like, I just, I just delayed in that.
你那种死守着必须维持结构平衡的执着，简直就像是，我当时就是，我就是在那儿耽搁了。

1503
01:14:30.480 --> 01:14:31.800
I remember that scene.
我记得那个场景。

1504
01:14:31.800 --> 01:14:39.640
I remember actually talking to you about the dialogue and you and Harold and, and I guess the first take I did it, maybe I didn't have that urgency.
我记得当时和你还有哈罗德讨论过那段台词，我想第一遍拍的时候，我可能没表现出那种紧迫感。

1505
01:14:39.640 --> 01:14:46.500
Like, and, and you guys like pulled me aside and you're like, no, like, like think like the buildings on fire.
就像，你们把我拉到一边说，不，你要想成是大楼着火了。

1506
01:14:46.600 --> 01:14:51.440
Like, you know, and that kind of like stakes.
就像，你知道，那种赌注。

1507
01:14:51.440 --> 01:15:02.980
And then Jen, while we're talking about this, I had a line where I said to Meredith, as she's deciding which party to go to, I say, Meredith, like, if you go to that party,
还有珍，说到这个，我当时有句台词是对梅雷迪思说的，当时她正在决定去参加哪个派对，我说，梅雷迪思，如果你去那个派对，

1508
01:15:03.080 --> 01:15:04.680
you're going to be very, very sorry.
你会非常非常后悔的。

1509
01:15:05.120 --> 01:15:06.080
I say something like that.
我大概是这么说的。

1510
01:15:06.240 --> 01:15:08.360
And then Meredith is like, is that a threat?
然后梅雷迪思问，这是威胁吗？

1511
01:15:08.720 --> 01:15:11.800
And I go, no, it's an invitation.
我说，不，这是邀请。

1512
01:15:11.800 --> 01:15:16.920
Like, I don't think I really got the joke on the first time I read it.
我第一次读剧本时好像没领会到这个笑点。

1513
01:15:17.040 --> 01:15:24.220
And you were like, Ange, were you like, maybe when you say it's an invitation, switch gears to like a smile.
然后你当时说，安吉，你是不是说，也许当你说是邀请时，换个语气，带点微笑。

1514
01:15:24.420 --> 01:15:28.340
And I remember it changed the whole scene.
我记得那改变了整个场景。

1515
01:15:28.620 --> 01:15:30.220
Like, I love that moment.
我喜欢那一刻。

1516
01:15:30.400 --> 01:15:38.320
That moment reminds me of in horror movies, when like the deranged ex-boyfriend is like, let me in this house right now.
那一刻让我想起恐怖电影里，那个精神失常的前男友说，现在就让我进屋。

1517
01:15:39.200 --> 01:15:41.300
And then, then there's like silence.
然后，接着就是一阵沉默。

1518
01:15:41.800 --> 01:15:45.800
And he's like, Monica, I'd love to come inside.
然后他说，莫妮卡，我很想进去。

1519
01:15:46.480 --> 01:15:49.020
And you're like, whoa, that is super creepy.
你会觉得，哇，这太毛骨悚然了。

1520
01:15:50.080 --> 01:15:52.520
That's scarier than the banging on the door.
那比砸门更可怕。

1521
01:15:53.200 --> 01:15:55.540
So that's when I watched that scene, Angela.
所以当我看到那个场景时，安吉拉。

1522
01:15:55.780 --> 01:15:58.680
It's an invitation was like super unnerving.
那句“这是邀请”真的让人非常不安。

1523
01:15:59.160 --> 01:16:00.560
It was like, what just happened?
就像是，刚才发生了什么？

1524
01:16:01.040 --> 01:16:02.120
That was a note.
那是导演指导。

1525
01:16:02.260 --> 01:16:03.760
That was a note from Jen Salata.
那是珍·塞拉塔给的指导。

1526
01:16:04.040 --> 01:16:05.420
Just a peek into her brain.
窥探一下她的内心世界。

1527
01:16:07.740 --> 01:16:08.740
Maybe what?
也许什么？

1528
01:16:08.740 --> 01:16:19.120
But there's definitely an alternate reality where the office is a horror and Angela, there are moments where you're standing by the copier and then just, you know, jump out.
但肯定存在一个平行现实，办公室是一部恐怖片，安吉拉，有些时候你站在复印机旁，然后突然，你知道，跳出来吓人。

1529
01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:30.800
But I feel like there's something where you get to see Angela's struggle of her real feelings in the episode because they leak out all over the place.
但我感觉在这一集中，你能看到安吉拉在真实情感上的挣扎，因为它们到处流露出来。

1530
01:16:30.920 --> 01:16:32.740
But then the pop back to this is a work.
但随后又弹回到“这是工作”。

1531
01:16:32.860 --> 01:16:33.600
This is an office.
这是办公室。

1532
01:16:33.880 --> 01:16:34.760
We have to be professional.
我们必须保持专业。

1533
01:16:34.760 --> 01:16:45.160
So there's like, we get to see that, like, moment where you, where you, the threat comes out and then, you know, no, this is office decorum.
所以我们能看到那一刻，你，威胁脱口而出，然后，你知道，不，这是办公室礼仪。

1534
01:16:45.260 --> 01:16:46.440
Here I am as well as this person.
我在这里既是这个人，也是那个人。

1535
01:16:46.440 --> 01:16:50.160
It's so creepy because we just saw what you really are or what part of you is.
这太可怕了，因为我们刚刚看到了你真实的一面，或者说你的一部分。

1536
01:16:50.420 --> 01:16:50.800
Yeah.
是的。

1537
01:16:50.800 --> 01:16:55.180
I love this storyline, too, because it serves that whole purpose.
我也喜欢这个故事情节，因为它达到了整体目的。

1538
01:16:55.180 --> 01:17:01.400
And then it also serves the purpose of putting Pam and Karen together and freaking Jim out.
而且它还起到了让帕姆和凯伦聚在一起，并让吉姆抓狂的作用。

1539
01:17:01.700 --> 01:17:05.580
So that's why I, it's so rich with storyline.
所以这就是为什么，它充满了故事情节。

1540
01:17:06.020 --> 01:17:08.740
Should we head over to Benihana?
我们要去贝尼汉娜餐厅了吗？

1541
01:17:09.000 --> 01:17:13.140
Because, Jen, we were not there that day that you shot in the restaurant.
因为，珍，你们在餐厅拍摄那天我们不在场。

1542
01:17:13.140 --> 01:17:15.440
And there are so many delicious scenes.
那里有很多精彩的场景。

1543
01:17:15.980 --> 01:17:16.660
Delicious.
精彩。

1544
01:17:16.660 --> 01:17:20.840
I feel like that was a really good use of, is that like a metaphor?
我觉得这用得真好，这是个比喻吗？

1545
01:17:21.280 --> 01:17:22.780
Because Benihana serves food?
因为贝尼汉娜供应食物？

1546
01:17:23.060 --> 01:17:23.540
I don't know.
我不知道。

1547
01:17:23.640 --> 01:17:24.540
Oh, well.
噢，好吧。

1548
01:17:25.020 --> 01:17:30.100
I think that the idea of Benihana might have come up because it always cheered me up when I was a kid.
我想贝尼汉娜这个点子可能是因为我小时候去那里总能让我开心起来。

1549
01:17:30.100 --> 01:17:33.260
If I had been sick or if I got a good grade, we'd go to Benihana.
如果我生病了或者得了好成绩，我们就会去贝尼汉娜。

1550
01:17:33.500 --> 01:17:37.000
And I remember they used to, like, fling shrimp in your mouth.
我记得他们以前会把虾扔进你嘴里。

1551
01:17:37.180 --> 01:17:41.800
I heard that when I was doing research for this episode that they stopped doing that.
我在为这一集做调研时听说，他们已经不再那样做了。

1552
01:17:41.960 --> 01:17:44.500
I think that they were worried about it being dangerous.
我想他们是担心那样做有危险。

1553
01:17:44.500 --> 01:17:47.960
And so, flinging hot shrimp across a restaurant.
所以，在餐厅里甩飞热虾什么的。

1554
01:17:48.620 --> 01:17:53.200
But, Jen, I love to know, like, how Benihana was, like, part of your childhood memory.
但是，珍，我很想知道，红花铁板烧（Benihana）是怎么成为你童年记忆的一部分的？

1555
01:17:53.300 --> 01:17:59.840
Because our youngest son, when he turned eight, the one thing he wanted to do was go to Benihana.
因为我们的小儿子八岁生日时，他唯一想做的事就是去红花铁板烧。

1556
01:18:00.240 --> 01:18:01.360
Oh, yes!
哦，没错！

1557
01:18:01.640 --> 01:18:03.780
That was, like, our eight-year-old's birthday.
那就像是我们八岁孩子的生日标配。

1558
01:18:03.980 --> 01:18:05.660
It's dinner and a show!
既能吃晚餐，又有表演看！

1559
01:18:05.660 --> 01:18:14.440
So, I remember, so, there was a scene where Dwight talks about the knife that the chef is using and how,
我记得，有一场戏是德怀特在谈论厨师用的刀，还有关于……

1560
01:18:14.600 --> 01:18:16.800
oh, I bet you're bummed that you want this other knife.
哦，我敢打赌你一定很沮丧，因为你想要另一把刀。

1561
01:18:16.880 --> 01:18:19.240
And the chef is saying, no, no, this knife is better.
而厨师说，不，不，这把刀更好。

1562
01:18:19.380 --> 01:18:21.080
Dwight, you don't know what the best knife is.
德怀特，你根本不懂什么是最好的刀。

1563
01:18:22.360 --> 01:18:27.500
And I kind of put this together when I was re-watching the episode.
我在重温这一集时，把这些细节串联了起来。

1564
01:18:28.080 --> 01:18:32.960
But when we were there, there was a problem making the onion volcano.
但我们当时在现场时，洋葱火山的制作出了点问题。

1565
01:18:33.500 --> 01:18:38.380
So, that was sort of the biggest holdup of the day.
那是那天拍摄最大的阻碍。

1566
01:18:39.820 --> 01:18:42.260
The chef couldn't make the onion volcano.
厨师做不出洋葱火山。

1567
01:18:42.380 --> 01:18:45.860
And then they brought in another chef to make the onion volcano.
后来他们又找来另一位厨师来做洋葱火山。

1568
01:18:46.100 --> 01:18:46.440
Oh, no.
哦，天哪。

1569
01:18:47.080 --> 01:18:50.020
We couldn't get an onion volcano off the ground.
我们当时怎么也做不出洋葱火山。

1570
01:18:50.180 --> 01:18:55.540
It was, and I think we had one of the more celebrated onion volcano chefs there.
而且我觉得我们当时请的还是最出名的洋葱火山厨师之一。

1571
01:18:56.000 --> 01:19:01.420
And what immediately happened to me, even though I wrote the scene with Dwight talking about knives,
我当时的第一反应是，尽管那场戏里德怀特谈论刀具的台词是我写的，

1572
01:19:01.460 --> 01:19:04.360
is I was like, well, maybe, is the oven hot enough?
但我当时想的是，嗯，也许，烤箱温度够吗？

1573
01:19:04.460 --> 01:19:06.840
Are you sure that the right positioning of the onions?
你确定洋葱摆放的位置对吗？

1574
01:19:06.980 --> 01:19:09.000
And I'm like, they know onion volcanoes.
然后我又想，人家才是洋葱火山专家。

1575
01:19:09.060 --> 01:19:10.940
I don't know onion volcanoes.
我根本不懂洋葱火山。

1576
01:19:10.940 --> 01:19:13.080
But we were desperately trying to get this.
但我们当时拼命想把这个镜头拍好。

1577
01:19:13.080 --> 01:19:20.680
And so, all of us were sitting around giving notes to a person who was already had a difficult time making the onion volcano,
所以，我们所有人都围在那儿，给一个本来就已经很难做出洋葱火山的人提意见，

1578
01:19:20.680 --> 01:19:22.380
trying to get help.
试图提供帮助。

1579
01:19:22.620 --> 01:19:23.860
You know, how can we help this?
你知道的，我们能怎么帮上忙呢？

1580
01:19:24.120 --> 01:19:29.900
So, secret little fun fact, the steam from the onion was added in poSt.
所以，告诉你们一个有趣的小秘密，洋葱冒出的蒸汽是后期制作加上去的。

1581
01:19:30.240 --> 01:19:31.680
Because we never...
因为我们从来没……

1582
01:19:31.680 --> 01:19:31.730
Oh, my gosh!
天哪！

1583
01:19:32.140 --> 01:19:34.860
The volcano never erupted on the day.
那天火山根本就没喷发。

1584
01:19:35.540 --> 01:19:45.700
There was, I think, a cluttering of smoke that came out after, I want to say, hours of trying to get this off the ground.
我想在尝试了几个小时之后，只冒出了一点点烟雾。

1585
01:19:45.960 --> 01:19:50.920
And I think it was Dean Holland who was editing the episode who's like, here's what we're going to do.
负责剪辑这一集的迪恩·霍兰德当时说，我们这样处理吧。

1586
01:19:51.660 --> 01:19:54.300
We're going to add some smoke to the onion.
我们给洋葱加点烟雾效果。

1587
01:19:54.340 --> 01:19:55.400
We can do this.
我们能搞定。

1588
01:19:55.400 --> 01:20:05.700
And I can just imagine my past Jen crying at that moment of relief because the onion sputtering would not lift up a heartbroken Michael.
我能想象出当时的珍在那一刻如释重负地哭了，因为洋葱如果只是冒点烟，是无法让心碎的迈克尔振作起来的。

1589
01:20:06.040 --> 01:20:09.320
And, you know, we needed that onion to do its part.
而且，你知道，我们需要那个洋葱发挥它的作用。

1590
01:20:10.060 --> 01:20:10.500
Yes.
是的。

1591
01:20:10.720 --> 01:20:11.160
Yes.
是的。

1592
01:20:11.740 --> 01:20:12.320
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

1593
01:20:12.320 --> 01:20:13.660
I love that story so much.
我太喜欢这个故事了。

1594
01:20:14.220 --> 01:20:21.540
So, Jen, now we want to talk about the storyline with the Benihana waitresses and how Michael can't tell them apart.
那么，珍，现在我们想聊聊关于红花铁板烧女服务员的那条故事线，以及迈克尔为什么分不清她们。

1595
01:20:21.540 --> 01:20:23.920
And I know we traded emails about this.
我知道我们为此交换过邮件。

1596
01:20:24.080 --> 01:20:30.380
Even though we're saying, Michael, you should know better what you're doing is wrong, is it even right to put that storyline out there?
尽管我们一直在说，迈克尔，你应该懂事点，你做的事情是错的，但把那样的故事线放出来真的合适吗？

1597
01:20:30.680 --> 01:20:33.100
Can you share your reaction to rewatching it?
你能分享一下你重温那段剧情时的感受吗？

1598
01:20:33.280 --> 01:20:37.740
You know, it's like I feel conflicted about the whole, like, there was this, like, Archie Bunker, Michael Scott,
你知道，我对此感到很矛盾，就像那种阿奇·邦克、迈克尔·斯科特，

1599
01:20:37.900 --> 01:20:41.960
David Brent thing that we would make a joke that this is clearly inappropriate.
还有大卫·布伦特式的梗，我们开玩笑说这显然是不合适的。

1600
01:20:41.960 --> 01:20:46.820
But some of that stuff is just not appropriate, even if it's being made a joke fun of.
但其中一些内容确实不妥，即使它是被当作笑话来处理的。

1601
01:20:46.820 --> 01:20:50.820
And some is, and I don't know where some of the line is, but what is what is offensive?
有些内容确实是，我不知道界限在哪里，但到底什么是冒犯？

1602
01:20:50.820 --> 01:20:52.440
What is going to hurt somebody?
什么会伤害到别人？

1603
01:20:52.640 --> 01:20:55.520
What is making fun of a character who does this?
什么是对做出这种行为的角色进行嘲讽？

1604
01:20:55.740 --> 01:20:57.220
And is that even OK?
这真的可以吗？

1605
01:20:57.340 --> 01:20:58.240
Or where's the line for that?
或者说这其中的界限在哪里？

1606
01:20:58.400 --> 01:21:08.240
We're constantly growing and have to understand, you know, even if our intention was to do something to show how ridiculous this behavior is at the time,
我们一直在成长，必须明白，即使我们当时的初衷是为了展示这种行为有多荒谬，

1607
01:21:08.320 --> 01:21:10.540
how does that portrayal come across?
那种刻画给人的感觉如何？

1608
01:21:10.540 --> 01:21:20.240
And is that affecting people and just taking it all in and just trying to educate myself as much as I can about how what we put out there affects people?
它是否影响到了人们？我只是在全盘吸收，并尽可能多地去了解我们发布的内容是如何影响他人的。

1609
01:21:20.720 --> 01:21:21.720
Yeah, absolutely.
是的，绝对是。

1610
01:21:21.720 --> 01:21:29.100
I feel like as a creative community, we need to learn and and reevaluate how we tell stories.
我觉得作为一个创意群体，我们需要学习并重新评估我们讲述故事的方式。

1611
01:21:29.460 --> 01:21:29.700
Yeah.
是的。

1612
01:21:30.060 --> 01:21:33.460
And and like you said, Jen, how those stories affect people.
就像你说的，Jen，这些故事是如何影响人们的。

1613
01:21:33.660 --> 01:21:33.820
Yeah.
是的。

1614
01:21:34.460 --> 01:21:35.400
All right, Jen.
好的，Jen。

